## Why This Is Asked
This question probes understanding of cache invalidation, versioned keys, outbox patterns, and end-to-end consistency in CNPA stacks. It also checks how to observe impact and plan safe rollouts.

## Key Concepts
- Cache invalidation strategies and TTLs
- Redis key versioning and cache-aside pattern
- Event-driven invalidation via Kafka
- Safe reads with read-fallbacks and refresh
- Observability of latency and hit ratios

## Code Example
```javascript
// Update path
BEGIN;
UPDATE profiles SET name=$1, email=$2, version = version + 1 WHERE id=$id;
COMMIT;
redis.del(`profile:${id}:v*`);
publishKafka('profile_updated', { id, version: NEW_VERSION });
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you test race conditions when read happens concurrently with update?
- How would you tune TTLs and decide when to prune old keys?

## Why This Is Asked

This question tests the ability to guarantee reliable cross-system delivery without distributed transactions, leveraging the Outbox pattern, idempotent consumption, and robust validation under partial failures.

## Key Concepts

- Outbox pattern
- Exactly-once delivery
- Idempotent consumers
- Durable Kafka keys
- Redis deduplication and caching

## Code Example

```sql
-- PostgreSQL tables
CREATE TABLE users (
  id UUID PRIMARY KEY,
  email TEXT,
  created_at TIMESTAMPTZ DEFAULT now()
);

CREATE TABLE outbox (
  id BIGINT GENERATED ALWAYS AS IDENTITY PRIMARY KEY,
  aggregate_id UUID,
  event_type TEXT,
  payload JSONB,
  processed BOOLEAN DEFAULT FALSE,
  created_at TIMESTAMPTZ DEFAULT now(),
  last_attempt TIMESTAMPTZ
);
```

```sql
-- Atomic signup write
BEGIN;
INSERT INTO users (id, email) VALUES (?, ?);
INSERT INTO outbox (aggregate_id, event_type, payload) VALUES (?, 'UserSignedUp', to_jsonb(?));
COMMIT;
```

